course: arts
lesson: 42
title: Olympiad: picture puzzles, colours, dates and myths ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: This olympiad block mixes the art lessons into puzzles: chains of colour mixing, putting famous paintings in order of time, counting on a drawing grid, and catching myths about art and buildings.
explain: Colour chains need two steps: first work out what a mix makes, then use the colour wheel, where opposite colours are complementary: red and green, blue and orange, yellow and purple.
explain: Date puzzles use simple sums: if Van Gogh was born in 1853 and painted The Starry Night in 1889, he was 36 then, and putting paintings in order needs only their years side by side.
explain: Picture Dad at the Louvre saying, "We'll see the whole lot before lunch, easy." That is hyperbole of a kind: no one can see everything in one day, and tired legs and eyes have a name, museum fatigue.
explain: People react in their own words: "mid" means so-so, "lowkey" means a bit or quietly, an Australian "heaps" means lots, and a Scottish "dreich" means grey and gloomy weather.
fact: Painters' primary colours are red, yellow and blue; the secondary colours green, orange and purple are made by mixing two of them, and mixing complementary colours gives a muddy brown or grey.
fact: Adding white to a colour makes a lighter tint, and adding black makes a darker shade.
fact: Dates of famous works: the Mona Lisa, early 1500s; the Sistine Chapel ceiling, 1508 to 1512; Girl with a Pearl Earring, around 1665; The Great Wave, around 1831; The Starry Night, 1889; The Scream, from 1893; Guernica, 1937.
fact: Vincent van Gogh was born in 1853 and died in 1890 aged 37; he sold very few paintings, and his fame grew after his death.
fact: The Great Wave off Kanagawa by Hokusai is a woodblock print, so many copies were made from the carved blocks.
fact: The rule of thirds splits a picture with two lines across and two lines down into a grid of 9 boxes, with 4 points where the lines cross.
fact: On a real head the eyes sit about halfway down; in perspective far things look smaller, and parallel lines seem to meet at a vanishing point.
fact: The Mona Lisa is about 77 centimetres tall and 53 centimetres wide; Guernica is almost 8 metres wide.
fact: Michelangelo painted the Sistine Chapel ceiling standing on a scaffold and bending his head back, not lying down.
fact: The Great Wall of China is very long but only about as wide as a road, so it cannot be seen from the Moon with the naked eye.
fact: Red-green colour blindness affects about 1 in 12 men of European descent.
fact: There is no rule that changing 30 percent of someone's picture makes it yours; what matters is whether the original can still be recognised.
exercise: Mia mixes red and yellow, then stirs in the colour opposite that result on the colour wheel. What does she get? | A muddy brown or grey | A clean, bright green | A rich, dark purple | Pure white, like light
exercise: Which secondary colour has no red in it at all? | Green | Orange | Purple | Pink
exercise: Blue and red are mixed. Which colour is the complementary of the result? | Yellow | Green | Orange | Blue
exercise: Grace adds white to the complementary colour of orange. What has she made? | A light blue, which is a tint | A dark blue, which is a shade | A light orange, which is a tint | A grey, which is a shade
exercise: Put these in order, oldest first: The Starry Night, the Mona Lisa, The Great Wave, Guernica. | Mona Lisa, Great Wave, Starry Night, Guernica | Mona Lisa, Starry Night, Great Wave, Guernica | Great Wave, Mona Lisa, Guernica, Starry Night | Starry Night, Mona Lisa, Great Wave, Guernica
exercise: Van Gogh was born in 1853. How old was he when he painted The Starry Night? | 36 | 37 | 27 | 26
exercise: Which came first: Girl with a Pearl Earring or the Sistine Chapel ceiling? | The ceiling, finished about 150 years earlier | The Girl, painted about 150 years earlier | They were both painted in the very same year | The ceiling, finished about 15 years earlier
exercise: Kai draws the rule-of-thirds grid on his photo. How many points does he get where the lines cross? | 4 | 9 | 6 | 3
exercise: How many boxes does the rule-of-thirds grid make? | 9 | 3 | 4 | 6
exercise: Zara's head drawing is 20 centimetres tall. About where should the eyes go? | Halfway, about 10 centimetres down | About 2 centimetres down from the top | About 5 centimetres down from the top | About 18 centimetres down from the top
exercise: About how many Mona Lisas, side by side, would stretch across Guernica? | About 15 | About 2 | About 150 | About 1,500
exercise: Odd one out: The Great Wave, The Starry Night, Guernica, The Scream. Which one, and why? | The Great Wave, a woodblock print | The Starry Night, painted in a hospital | Guernica, because it is in black, white and grey | The Scream, because it has more than one version
exercise: Ella's brother says Michelangelo painted the ceiling lying on his back. What is true? | He stood on a scaffold, bending his head back | He lay on his back on a soft mattress | He painted it on the floor and lifted it up | He painted it from a ladder in a single day
exercise: Which of these is the myth? | The Great Wall can be seen from the Moon | Pisa's tower leans because soft ground sank | The Taj Mahal was built as a tomb | The Eiffel Tower was meant to stand 20 years
exercise: Two rows of the same trees line a straight road to the horizon. In a good drawing, which tree is drawn smallest? | The one farthest away, near the vanishing point | The one nearest the person who is drawing | The one exactly in the middle of the row | All the same size, just as in real life
exercise: Van Gogh died in 1890. How many years after The Scream was first made did he die? | None: he died 3 years before it | 3 years after it was first made | 37 years after it was first made | Exactly 1 year after it was made
test: "lowkey my sky looks mid so I added black to make it brighter" ("mid" means so-so). What would really help? | Darker colours beside the sky, for contrast | More black, mixed right into the blue | White mixed into everything, sky and land | Leaving it overnight so it dries brighter
test: Nan reckons The Starry Night was painted after Van Gogh died, "cos he only got famous later". What is the truth? | He painted it in 1889 and died in 1890; the fame came after | She is right, friends painted it from his notes in 1891 | She is right, it was painted by his brother Theo in 1900 | He painted it in 1853, the year he was born, as a baby
test: Ollie draws the far-away people bigger "so you can see them properly". What happens to his picture? | It loses its depth, because far things should look smaller | It looks deeper and much more real than before | Nothing changes, since size has nothing to do with distance | The near people now look far away and the far ones near
test: Josh says, "Guernica's literally the size of a football pitch." What is the real size? | Almost 8 metres wide: big, but nowhere near a pitch | Exactly the size of a football pitch, about 100 metres | About 77 centimetres, the same as the Mona Lisa | Smaller than a postage stamp, but it looks big in photos
test: Dad: "The Louvre? We'll see the whole lot before lunch, easy." What is likely to happen? | They see a little and get museum fatigue | They see every single object by lunchtime | They finish the whole museum in ten minutes | They are not allowed to look at more than one room
test: Put the steps of drawing a head in order. | Light big shapes, then construction lines, then details, then shading | Shading, then details, then big shapes, then construction lines | Details, then shading, then big shapes, then construction lines | Big shapes, then shading, then construction lines, then details
test: A painting class has 24 boys of European descent. About how many might have red-green colour blindness? | 2 | 12 | 1 | 24
test: The art teacher looks at Kai's photo with the horizon dead in the middle and says, "Hmm, not ideal." What does she really mean? | Move the horizon onto a third line | The photo is perfect, leave it exactly as it is | The photo is so bad that it must be deleted at once | The horizon should go right at the very top edge
test: Tom from Glasgow says it's dreich ("dreich" means grey and gloomy) and paints the sky pure grey. His friend says, "Shadows and greys can have colour in them." Who is right? | The friend: greys and shadows often hold blues and purples | Tom: grey skies have no colour in them at all | Tom: real painters only use black and white for weather | The friend: any colour at all, like bright pink, looks right
test: Sophie says, "Change 30 percent of a picture and it's yours." Is that a real rule? | No: what matters is whether the original is still recognisable | Yes: 30 percent is the official rule for every country | Yes: but only 10 percent needs changing for drawings | No: the rule is really 50 percent, not 30 percent
test: An Aussie kid says Van Gogh painted heaps ("heaps" means lots) of sunflowers in 1888 and 1889. Were they all fresh? | No, they go from fresh to wilting | Yes, every one is a fresh, open flower | No, they were all dead, dried flowers | Yes, he only painted plastic sunflowers
test: A pavement painting looks like a deep hole, but only from one spot. Why? | It uses perspective tricks that work from one viewpoint | The chalk has dug a real hole into the pavement | The hole is real, and the chalk only colours its sides | People see it from one spot because the rest is fenced off
exam: Determine the colour obtained when white is added to the complementary colour of purple. | A light yellow, which is a tint | A dark yellow, which is a shade | A light green, which is a tint | A light purple, which is a tint
exam: Select the arrangement of works in correct chronological order, the earliest first. | Sistine Chapel ceiling, Girl with a Pearl Earring, The Great Wave, Guernica | Girl with a Pearl Earring, Sistine Chapel ceiling, Guernica, The Great Wave | The Great Wave, Sistine Chapel ceiling, Guernica, Girl with a Pearl Earring | Guernica, The Great Wave, Girl with a Pearl Earring, Sistine Chapel ceiling
exam: Ascertain the number of intersections produced by the grid employed in the rule of thirds. | Four | Nine | Three | Six
exam: Which of the following statements concerning the Great Wall of China is consistent with the established facts? | It is too narrow to be seen from the Moon with the naked eye | It is clearly visible from the Moon with the naked eye | It is the only human structure visible from the Moon | It is as wide as a motorway along its whole length
